<section class="sub" id="ldc-concepts">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">CONCEPTS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Seven approaches to land development code reform</h2>
<p class="section-note">Each concept is a tool for restructuring how code is written or how permits get decided — independent of what uses or densities a city ultimately allows.</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list">
<article class="card case-study" id="ldc-form-based">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Form-Based Code</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Code structure</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Regulates the physical form of buildings — height, setbacks, frontage, how a building meets the street — rather than separating land uses into a table of permitted and prohibited categories. A regulating plan or map assigns each area a place on a "transect" from rural to urban intensity, and many uses can mix freely as long as the building's form fits its zone.</p>
<span class="field-label">Examples</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="/cities/flagstaff/">Flagstaff, AZ</a> — its state-mandated middle-housing code builds on a code that was already form-based, per this library's own profile.</li>
<li><a href="/cities/miami/">Miami 21 (Miami, FL)</a> — adopted 2010; described by its authors as the largest known application of a transect-based form code in a U.S. city, replacing Miami's use-based zoning citywide.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">Outcomes reported for Miami 21 are mostly qualitative — more predictable review, a more transparent process for landowners — rather than measured before/after statistics. The code has also seen amendments over time that some legal reviewers argue diluted its original form-based intent.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.dpz.com/projects/miami-21/">DPZ — Miami 21 project overview ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.miami21.org/PDFs/FinalDocumentsMay2010/FULLDOCUMENT-May2010.pdf">Miami 21 — full code text (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ldc-unified-code">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Unified / Consolidated Development Code</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Code structure</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Merges zoning, subdivision regulation, and design standards — historically written and amended as separate chapters or ordinances — into a single, internally consistent code. Often done alongside a comprehensive or general plan update, so the code directly implements the plan rather than trailing behind it.</p>
<span class="field-label">Examples</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="/cities/charlotte/">Charlotte, NC</a> — its Unified Development Ordinance (UDO), already profiled in this library, allows duplex/triplex/quadplex by right.</li>
<li><a href="/cities/columbus/">Columbus, OH</a> — mid-rewrite of its entire zoning code via "Zone In," also already profiled in this library.</li>
<li>Louisville / Jefferson County, KY — consolidated zoning, form districts, subdivision, transportation, parking, and design standards into one Land Development Code following its 2003 city-county merger.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">A full-code consolidation is a large, multi-year undertaking — exactly the scale of project Santa Fe is already underway with, pairing its General Plan and Land Development Code updates rather than doing them years apart.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.planning.org/publications/document/9027800/">American Planning Association — "The Sensibility of Unified Development Codes" ↗</a>
<a href="https://louisvilleky.gov/government/office-planning/land-development-code">Louisville Metro — Land Development Code ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ldc-objective-standards">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Objective, Numeric Development Standards</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Code structure</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Replaces subjective criteria — "compatible with neighborhood character," "high-quality design" — with measurable numbers: a specific height in feet, a setback distance, a lot-coverage percentage. A project either meets the number or it doesn't, which is what lets a staff planner check it against a list rather than sending it to a hearing.</p>
<span class="field-label">Examples</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="/cities/spokane/">Spokane, WA</a> — its dimensional-standards table, already profiled in this library as the model for the kind of table Santa Fe's own code currently leaves blank.</li>
<li>Menifee, CA — adopted objective design standards for multifamily housing in 2022, to comply with a state-law requirement that housing meeting objective standards can't be denied or delayed on subjective grounds.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">A widely cited study of <a href="/cities/seattle/">Seattle</a>'s discretionary, board-based design review found it added an average of 11 months to the permit timeline, with one 57-unit project delayed 16 months over façade disputes — illustrating the cost of the alternative rather than a flaw in objective standards themselves. Writing standards specific enough to count as legally "objective" takes real upfront staff work.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.sightline.org/2017/09/06/how-seattles-design-review-sabotages-housing-affordability/">Sightline Institute — Seattle design-review delay study ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.cityofmenifee.us/DocumentCenter/View/15001/Final-Adopted-ODS_4-6-22">City of Menifee — adopted Objective Design Standards (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ldc-by-right">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>By-Right / Ministerial Approval</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Approval process</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>A project that meets every written standard is approved administratively by staff — no discretionary public hearing, no elected-body vote, and, where paired with objective standards, no case-by-case argument about whether it "fits." Approval becomes a matter of checking boxes rather than persuading a board.</p>
<span class="field-label">Examples</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="/cities/california/">California SB 35 / AB 1287</a> — ministerial approval for qualifying infill housing, already profiled in this library.</li>
<li><a href="/cities/austin/">Austin, TX</a> — Affordability Unlocked and HOME both remove the rezoning-and-hearing step for qualifying projects.</li>
<li><a href="/cities/charlotte/">Charlotte, NC</a> — its UDO allows duplex, triplex, and quadplex construction by right.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">A study of 352 multifamily projects in Los Angeles found by-right approvals were 60 to 248 days faster than discretionary ones, depending on location — but by-right review also forecloses the exactions (affordable units, public space, infrastructure) a discretionary hearing can extract project by project. Whether that trade is worth it is a policy choice, not a settled question.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.planning.org/blog/9272821/discretionary-vs-by-right-approval-which-is-faster/">American Planning Association — discretionary vs. by-right approval speed ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ldc-pre-approved-plans">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Pre-Approved Plans &amp; Pattern Books</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Approval process</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>A government pre-reviews a set of standard building plans — for an ADU, a duplex, a cottage — once, in advance. A property owner who picks a pre-approved plan skips individual design review entirely, cutting both permitting time and the cost of hiring an architect for a one-off design.</p>
<span class="field-label">Examples</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="/cities/southbend/">South Bend, IN</a> — its pre-approved infill design catalog, already profiled in this library.</li>
<li>California AB 1332 (2023) — requires every city and county in the state to run a pre-approved ADU plan program by January 2025, with a 30-day decision deadline once a plan is submitted.</li>
<li>San Jose, CA — runs a program with plan sets from multiple vendors pre-approved, advertising same-day permit issuance for sites that meet the program's site criteria.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">Pre-approved plans only work for sites matching the plan's assumptions — standard lot size and shape, no hazard overlays, typical utility connections. An unusual lot still needs full custom review.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240AB1332">AB 1332 (2023) — bill text ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.sanjoseca.gov/business/development-services-permit-center/accessory-dwelling-units-adus/preapproved-adus">City of San José — pre-approved ADU program ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ldc-hearing-examiners">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Independent Hearing Examiners</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Decision-making</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>A trained, appointed officer — not an elected council member or a volunteer commissioner — decides quasi-judicial land-use matters: variances, conditional-use permits, sometimes plat approvals or appeals. The idea is to separate individual project decisions, which turn on whether a specific proposal meets specific standards, from the political pressure an elected body faces on a contested local project.</p>
<span class="field-label">Examples</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li>Washington State — authorizes this by statute for both code cities (RCW 35A.63.170) and other cities and counties (RCW 35.63.130).</li>
<li>King County, WA — its independent hearing examiner, appointed by the county council, decides permit appeals, subdivisions, and code-enforcement appeals.</li>
<li>Bellevue, WA — runs a three-track system: the examiner makes the final decision on conditional-use permits and plats, hears appeals of administrative decisions, and issues a recommendation — not a final decision — to city council on project-specific rezones.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">This is best documented as a Washington State model in the sources reviewed for this library; a comparably well-documented example outside Washington wasn't confirmed and isn't asserted here. Moving decisions to an appointed officer also means individual project decisions carry less direct electoral accountability — a structural trade-off, not an empirical one.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/rcw/default.aspx?cite=35A.63.170">RCW 35A.63.170 — hearing examiner system ↗</a>
<a href="https://kingcounty.gov/en/independents/governance-and-leadership/government-oversight/hearing-examiner/about">King County — Hearing Examiner's Office ↗</a>
<a href="https://bellevuewa.gov/city-government/departments/city-clerks-office/hearing-examiners-office/How-it-Works">Bellevue — Hearing Examiner's Office ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ldc-shot-clock">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Expedited Review Deadlines ("Shot Clocks")</h3>
<span class="cs-tag">Approval process</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>A statutory or ordinance deadline requires an approval or denial within a fixed number of days of a complete application. If the deadline passes without action, the application is automatically — "deemed" — approved, removing the option to delay a project indefinitely just by not deciding.</p>
<span class="field-label">Examples</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li>California ADU law (Gov. Code §65852.2) — requires a decision within 60 days of a complete application, or the application is deemed approved — one of the most litigation-tested shot-clock provisions in U.S. housing law.</li>
<li>California AB 2234 (2022) — a separate, later-stage shot clock for post-entitlement building permits: a 15-day completeness check, then 30 days to decide for projects of 25 units or fewer and 60 days for larger ones, phasing in for bigger jurisdictions starting 2024 and smaller ones by 2028.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">A deemed-approval deadline creates real risk for an under-resourced planning department — falling behind on staffing can mean projects get approved without the review they were supposed to receive. Disputes over what counts as a "complete" application are also a recurring source of litigation.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://law.justia.com/codes/california/2023/code-gov/title-7/division-1/chapter-4/article-2/section-65852-2/">Cal. Gov. Code §65852.2 — text ↗</a>
<a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202120220AB2234">AB 2234 (2022) — bill text ↗</a></div>
</article>
</div>
